  [1mSection 2 — Data model and notification lifecycle[0m

  [1mTask record[0m (the shape [38;5;153mnotifications.js[39m reads — the one thing that's expensive to change later):

  { id, title, [36mdone[39m: [34mfalse[39m, [36mdueAt[39m: [34mnull[39m, [36mwatched[39m: [34mfalse[39m, createdAt }
  [38;5;153mdueAt[39m is epoch ms or [38;5;153mnull[39m. [38;5;153mwatched[39m is your "cares about" flag — explicit, per task, off by default.

  [1mDerived notification[0m (never persisted):
  { [36mid[39m: [31m`[39m${taskId}:${kind}`, taskId, title, [36mkind[39m: [31m'due-soon'[39m | [31m'overdue'[39m, dueAt }

  [1mThe rules[0m, all inside [38;5;153mcomputeNotifications(tasks, now, acks)[39m:
  - Ignore any task that is unwatched, done, or has no [38;5;153mdueAt[39m.
  - [38;5;153mnow >= dueAt[39m → [1moverdue[0m. Otherwise [38;5;153mdueAt - now <= 60min[39m → [1mdue-soon[0m.
  - At most one notification per task; overdue supersedes due-soon.
  - Sort by [38;5;153mdueAt[39m ascending. Unread count is just the length.

  [1mDismissal[0m is the only persisted notification state, and it has one subtlety worth calling out: dismissing "due in an
  hour" must [3mnot[0m swallow the later "overdue" — that's the failure mode that makes people stop trusting a notification
  bell. Separate ack keys per kind handle it.

  Acks are stored as [38;5;153m{ dismissedAt, dueAtWhenDismissed }[39m, and an ack is ignored if [38;5;153mdueAtWhenDismissed[39m no longer matches
  the task's current [38;5;153mdueAt[39m. So rescheduling a task automatically re-arms its notifications with no mutation hooks
  anywhere — the staleness fix stays inside the same pure function rather than leaking into the CRUD layer. Acks for
  deleted tasks get pruned on save.

  [1mRecompute[0m on three triggers: a 30-second tick, any task mutation, and [38;5;153mvisibilitychange[39m. That last one matters —
  browsers throttle timers in background tabs to roughly once a minute, so returning to the tab must recompute
  immediately rather than render a stale badge.

  Known limitation I'd accept rather than solve: two tabs open means last-write-wins on [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. Fixing it is the
  "changes since last visit" feature you already declined.

  Does the model and those rules look right?

